Our in-depth examination of the observed direction of extremely high-energy cosmic rays detected by the Pierre Auger Observatory reveals significant patterns of CR dissemination. Our study focuses on the most comprehensive dataset of cosmic-ray arrival directions ever collected, consisting of over 19,000 events exceeding 4 EeV detected with the Observatory's Cherenkov Technique and nearly 12,000 events detected with the Fluorescence Technique. We discovered renowned anisotropies in the sky, together with new, more precise measurements of their characteristics. The collective study of both datasets confirms the persistence of the "Octant Anisotropy," a long-observed phenomenon where more cosmic rays are found in the northern octants of the celestial sphere. We further found that the cosmic-ray hotspots: 
1) persist in their positions over nearly a decade, indicating their persistent nature;
2) exhibit differing degrees of activity, with some hotspots fading from previous surveys;
3) concentrate primarily near the Galactic plane, specifically within a longitudinal range of 20°, particularly in the outer Galaxy; 
4) display correlations with specific types of galaxies and quasars, specifically radio galaxies and blazars, although these correlations may result from statistical coincidences due to the vast number of sources in the sky. 
